{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y88jBUnHik5oy2Q91mtmuS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"DFT binding energy atomic oxygen Cu(111) fcc site eV PBE relative to gas-phase O atom\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914\"},{\"title\":\"par.nsf.gov\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10320492\"},{\"title\":\"s11433 012 4868 5\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface\",\"url\":\"https://archive.aps.org/mar/2022/g47/5\"},{\"title\":\"Structural and electronic properties of atomic oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) surface: A first-principles investigation\",\"url\":\"https://bvbvf1.bib-bvb.de/vufind/PrimoRecord/cdi_chongqing_primary_44769476\"},{\"title\":\"www.rsc.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.rsc.org/suppdata/c8/cp/c8cp04253e/c8cp04253e1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"qt8r80m1wb noSplash 0302f6f1aca79967d03eb36c7dc17dd0\",\"url\":\"https://escholarship.org/content/qt8r80m1wb/qt8r80m1wb_noSplash_0302f6f1aca79967d03eb36c7dc17dd0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface\",\"url\":\"https://https-meetings--archive-aps-org-443.webvpn1.xju.edu.cn/mar/2022/g47/5/\"},{\"title\":\"P 46 a11 6122919\",\"url\":\"https://accms.mobility.niche.tohoku.ac.jp/ACCMS-11/POSTER/P-46_a11_6122919.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"lian17 111101\",\"url\":\"https://www.henkelmanlab.org/pubs/lian17_111101.pdf\"}]\n\nThe best match is an arXiv paper (2505.12914) that reports PBE binding energies for a single O atom on Cu(111) and uses the gas-phase O atom as the reference. Its fcc hollow value is -5.000 eV and its hcp value is -4.905 eV. Threefold hollow sites, both fcc and hcp, are the energetically preferred binding sites for O*. The paper defines binding energy as the slab energy with adsorbed O minus the bare slab energy minus the number of adsorbed O atoms times the energy of an isolated gas-phase O atom, so negative values mean binding is favorable. It uses VASP with the PBE functional.\n\nA conflicting value comes from a conference poster (Matsuzaki and Nunomura), which reports -6.37 eV for fcc and -6.26 eV for hcp, and the fcc hollow site exhibiting slightly stronger binding than the hcp hollow site. The snippet does not state the reference energy or functional, so the gap of about 1.4 eV with the arXiv value likely reflects a different reference state or setup. I'd treat the arXiv numbers as the ones that match your reference definition, but I couldn't confirm that from these results.\n\nA separate paper (Chinese Science Bulletin, 2012) reports that the fcc-hollow site is the most stable site for oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) across coverages from 0.11 to 1.00 ML, but the snippet gives no eV values.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d73de39-d716-4340-ab2a-89f0bc9adf5d","uuid":"8458d444-4225-4905-8ae1-f541c2277bde","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:56:32.186Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"DFT binding energy atomic oxygen Cu(111) fcc site eV PBE relative to gas-phase O atom","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_012z7chvtAw9YT9UfMbSifHw","content":[{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914"},{"title":"par.nsf.gov","url":"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10320492"},{"title":"s11433 012 4868 5","url":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5.pdf"},{"title":"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface","url":"https://archive.aps.org/mar/2022/g47/5"},{"title":"Structural and electronic properties of atomic oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) surface: A first-principles investigation","url":"https://bvbvf1.bib-bvb.de/vufind/PrimoRecord/cdi_chongqing_primary_44769476"},{"title":"www.rsc.org","url":"https://www.rsc.org/suppdata/c8/cp/c8cp04253e/c8cp04253e1.pdf"},{"title":"qt8r80m1wb noSplash 0302f6f1aca79967d03eb36c7dc17dd0","url":"https://escholarship.org/content/qt8r80m1wb/qt8r80m1wb_noSplash_0302f6f1aca79967d03eb36c7dc17dd0.pdf"},{"title":"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface","url":"https://https-meetings--archive-aps-org-443.webvpn1.xju.edu.cn/mar/2022/g47/5/"},{"title":"P 46 a11 6122919","url":"https://accms.mobility.niche.tohoku.ac.jp/ACCMS-11/POSTER/P-46_a11_6122919.pdf"},{"title":"lian17 111101","url":"https://www.henkelmanlab.org/pubs/lian17_111101.pdf"}]},"The best match is an arXiv paper (2505.12914) that reports PBE binding energies for a single O atom on Cu(111) and uses the gas-phase O atom as the reference. Its fcc hollow value is -5.000 eV and its hcp value is -4.905 eV. Threefold hollow sites, both fcc and hcp, are the energetically preferred binding sites for O*. The paper defines binding energy as the slab energy with adsorbed O minus the bare slab energy minus the number of adsorbed O atoms times the energy of an isolated gas-phase O atom, so negative values mean binding is favorable. It uses VASP with the PBE functional.\n\nA conflicting value comes from a conference poster (Matsuzaki and Nunomura), which reports -6.37 eV for fcc and -6.26 eV for hcp, and the fcc hollow site exhibiting slightly stronger binding than the hcp hollow site. The snippet does not state the reference energy or functional, so the gap of about 1.4 eV with the arXiv value likely reflects a different reference state or setup. I'd treat the arXiv numbers as the ones that match your reference definition, but I couldn't confirm that from these results.\n\nA separate paper (Chinese Science Bulletin, 2012) reports that the fcc-hollow site is the most stable site for oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) across coverages from 0.11 to 1.00 ML, but the snippet gives no eV values."],"durationSeconds":6.655880562000005,"searchCount