{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgb4qQv5fztEQpx3nyptN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I calculated an adsorption energy of **−4.44 ± 0.6 eV** for atomic O on Cu(111), at its most stable site (the fcc hollow), referenced to an isolated O atom. I posted it to the board with a flag. The number is usable but less reliable than it looks, because of how the model handles the isolated-atom reference.\n\n**Calculation:** MACE-MP-0 (medium) on the GPU, 0 K geometry relaxations of a 3×3 Cu slab, 5 layers thick, bottom two layers fixed, with O at 1/9 monolayer.\n\n| Site | E_ads vs O atom (eV) |\n|---|---|\n| fcc hollow | **−4.436** (most stable) |\n| hcp hollow | −4.423 |\n| bridge | relaxes into the fcc site |\n| top | −2.54 |\n\n- **Convergence:** a smaller cell (2×2×4, 0.25 monolayer), 3×3×4 and 3×3×5 agree within 0.01 eV. A 4×4 run was still going when I posted and isn't included.\n- **Other reference:** against half an O₂ molecule (same model), the value is −1.67 eV.\n- **fcc vs hcp:** fcc wins by only 0.014 eV. That ordering agrees with DFT, but the gap is smaller than this model can reliably resolve.\n\n**Premise check:** this model learned from bulk-crystal DFT data and doesn't describe a free, spin-polarized O atom. Its isolated-atom energy is a fitted baseline rather than a physical energy. I flagged the result `isolated_atom_reference_outside_model_domain` instead of refusing, and added a second estimate that avoids the model's atom energy (below).\n\n**Validation:**\n- **Cu lattice constant:** 3.626 Å vs about 3.615 Å measured, within 0.3%. This passes.\n- **Cu cohesive energy:** a quantity that also uses the isolated-atom energy. The model gives 3.16 eV vs 3.49 eV measured, too weak by 0.33 eV. This confirms the problem.\n- **O₂ binding energy:** the model gives 5.53 eV. Measured is about 5.21 eV, and standard DFT (PBE) gives about 6.0–6.2 eV. The model matches neither.\n- **Literature DFT:** a PBE paper I found by web search reports fcc −5.00 / hcp −4.91 eV against an isolated O atom. That's about 0.56 eV stronger than my result, with the same site ordering. I only saw these figures in a search summary and didn't read the paper, so I couldn't confirm its oxygen coverage.\n\n**Uncertainty (±0.6 eV):** this spans three numbers:\n- my MACE value, −4.44 eV;\n- the alternative estimate, −4.28 eV: the model's half-O₂ result minus half the measured O₂ binding energy;\n- the DFT literature value, −5.00 eV.\n\nCell size and slab thickness add under 0.01 eV. Not included: differences between DFT functionals, zero-point energy (my rough estimate is about 0.06 eV), temperature, and coverages above 0.25 monolayer.\n\nBoard entries: candidate `e2d05567-74c1-4863-95ad-81dab0497e6b`, property result `05f4a958-c66f-4b5e-a824-1796b0a26142`.\n\nSources:\n- [arXiv:2505.12914](https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914)\n- [Matsuzaki & Nunomura poster (ACCMS-11)](https://accms.mobility.niche.tohoku.ac.jp/ACCMS-11/POSTER/P-46_a11_6122919.pdf)\n- [Sci. China paper on O/Cu(111) coverage](https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5.pdf)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1738,"cache_read_input_tokens":43366,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1738,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d73de39-d716-4340-ab2a-89f0bc9adf5d","uuid":"ede05594-3b98-47df-aad3-c89a0052432e","timestamp":"2026-10-31T22:00:35.146Z","request_id":"req_011Cgb4qQXWf63pvagG1Ssx6"}
